2.3 Upgrading in an existing Emacs session
==========================================

Previously compiled consumers and keymaps already created by
‘keymap-popup-define’, ‘keymap-popup-annotate’ or ‘keymap-popup-attach’
continue to work after loading the new library.  Recompilation or
reevaluation is not required to keep their descriptions, exit key, title
or persistence setting.  Existing map objects, anonymous commands,
custom bindings, parents and composed maps are retained.

   Legacy metadata is converted on first popup use, metadata access or
update, without rebuilding the keymap.  The converted metadata is inert
for native help and key lookup.  Until a retained map has been used by
the new library, native help can still show its old pseudo-key entries;
loading the library does not search all variables for keymaps.  Copies
made before or after conversion remain usable, and changing a copy's
popup descriptions does not change the original.

   Reevaluating a declaration refreshes its descriptions and options
while preserving existing bindings.  Reattaching runtime descriptions
replaces previous descriptions and options, with omitted options
returning to their defaults.  The private metadata representation is not
a public API, and loading an older library over converted maps is not
supported.


