6.7 Mouse activation
====================

A single left click on an enabled entry's key or description selects the
original source window and invokes its current key binding there,
without moving source point.  The action may then select another window
normally.  Pending prefix arguments, switches, submenus and ‘:stay-open’
work as with keyboard input.  Entry text uses native hover highlighting.

   Activation requires a matching left-button press and release on the
same entry in the same popup navigation state.  A release alone, or one
after replacing the popup or changing its submenu, does not activate an
entry.

   Headings, padding and blank space are inert.  Disabled or hidden
entries, changed bindings and drags do not activate an old action.
Popup clicks are ignored while a minibuffer prompt is active, including
presses whose release arrives after the prompt closes.  Inert clicks
leave the selected window unchanged; clicks outside the popup retain
their ordinary bindings.  If the source window no longer displays the
source buffer, mouse activation is refused rather than redirecting an
action to a different buffer.  Reinitializing the source's major mode or
changing its visited file retires the old popup's mouse activation; open
a new popup to use the new source context.  Renaming the buffer alone
does not retire it.

   The replay check runs before native transient-map teardown.  As with
typed keys, subsequent command hooks can still change the command's
buffer or other execution context.


